> **Scoring**: ❌ -1 point. The answer "icosahedron" is entirely unrelated to feasibility checking in trees.  
> **Feedback**: The response lacks relevance to the question. Below is an explanation of the correct concepts:  
>  
> **Recursive Traversal**:  
> - Traverses the tree from root to leaves, verifying constraints at each node.  
> - Example: Validating if a path meets all conditions (e.g., minimum/maximum edge weights).  
>  
> **Greedy Algorithm**:  
> - Chooses the best local action (e.g., selecting the "best" edge) at each step without revisiting nodes.  
> - Risk: May fail globally due to myopic decisions (e.g., choosing a path that blocks valid alternatives).  
>  
> **Key Difference**: Recursive ensures correctness but is computationally heavier; greedy is faster but may sacrifice optimality.  

